blobs.51e488748785:
﻿// The pattern's "Pad" part, as a GENERATED lane: the engine plays it from
// algo/pattern.strudel while the transport rolls, which is why it says `strudel:` where an
// authored lane says `events:`.
//
// Nothing here says what it carries — a lane is not typed, and each event leaves by the output
// its own material fits. What makes these notes is that the pattern names no sample and that
// graph.k7x wires `pad#midi:` to the instrument.
extends("@meta/strudel-track")
strudel("algo/pattern.strudel")

blobs.5270ebfc8ea2:
// The signal graph, as SEGMENTS: each module says where its signal goes next. The chains are
// DERIVED by following the arrows — a run breaks where the signal splits, and where two signals
// meet (which is what a bus is).
//
// The LANES are here too ("melody", "pad"): a lane is a module like any other, and a run is
// named after the lane that starts it. One map, one name space — which is why the pad's synth
// is "synth-pad" rather than sharing its lane's name.
//
// `bypassed` lists the modules the signal passes through untouched, and on a lane — which
// produces rather than passes — that is its mute.

// A lane offers TWO outputs, `audio` and `midi`, and is not typed by what it holds — so the
// key names the one a run leaves by whenever there is anything to say. "melody" holds only
// sequences, so its bare key is unambiguous; "pad" streams from a pattern and holds nothing
// until the transport rolls, so nothing but this line can say its events are notes for the
// synth. See packages/song/docs/trackModules.md.

// `MIX` is the mixer's sum and `OUT` is the interface; the run between them is the MASTER
// CHAIN, and `OUT` carries the song's output level as its `Volume`. Name a module on that
// line and it processes both lanes together on the way out.

modules({
  melody: ["fourosc"],
  "pad#midi": ["synth-pad"],
  lead: ["MIX"],
  synth-pad: ["MIX"],
  MIX: ["OUT"],
  fourosc: ["lead"],
})

blobs.53f96e9bb764:
﻿// The ARRANGEMENT — the timeline, and the transport that rolls through it.
//
// A project draws one because it says so here: the timeline is a SURFACE
// (packages/song/docs/surfaces.md), not something the editor hands out for free. Take this
// file away and the song still compiles and still plays — you simply have no lanes on screen.
//
// `split` is what puts it in the layout, sharing the window with the graph underneath, and is
// the only layout a timeline draws in. `view` is the other one: a screen of its own off the
// header, which is where a turntables desk goes.
type("timeline")
layout("split")

blobs.540e16f50271:
﻿// The B deck — backbeat. It lands on the crossfade's `b` port, which is why its chain ends
// there and it runs no modules of its own.
//
// BYPASSED on purpose, in chains.k7x, and this is the one thing about the demo worth reading
// twice.
//
// `out` compiles as a PARALLEL path in this compiler — that is what makes a send a send, the
// dry signal carrying on to the master beside it. A deck feeding only a crossfade's second port
// gets the same treatment, so unbypassed you hear deck B twice: once raw, once through the
// module, and pushing Mix to 0 does not take it away.
//
// Bypassing a LANE says "this lane is not heard on its own" — on a source, bypass means it
// produces nothing rather than passing something through. It works because Tracktion's muting
// node deliberately still lets clips through to aux sends and sidechains, so the crossfade keeps
// its second signal while the master stops getting a duplicate. Measured: with it, Mix 0 is the
// kick alone, Mix 1 the snare alone; without it, the snare is there at every setting. See
// packages/song/docs/ports.md.
//
// TODO: try automating Mix so the two decks trade places over a bar.
extends("@meta/track")
events([ref("snare").start("1:2:1"), ref("snare").start("1:4:1")])

